<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Association of Southeast Asian Nations</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ASEAN</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) is a regional organisation founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> in August 1967 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, Malaysia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It later admitted Brunei, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, and Timor-Leste became its eleventh member in 2025. It promotes economic cooperation, regional stability and non-interference among its members, with a secretariat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/association-of-southeast-asian-nations.jpg" alt="Association of Southeast Asian Nations" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ASEAN_HQ_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien III abdicated in favour of his son Hassanal Bolkiah in 1967, and Brunei became fully independent on 1 January 1984, joining <b>ASEAN</b> and the United Nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>…new state normalised relations with Indonesia in its first year, and Indonesia came to back its entry into <b>ASEAN</b>: applied for in 2011, accepted in principle in November 2022 and, as announced on 27 May 2025, completed at the October 2025 summit in Kuala Lumpur as the 11th…</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1967 Indonesia was a founder of the <b>Association of Southeast Asian Nations</b> (ASEAN), whose secretariat is in Jakarta, and its size makes it the anchor of regional diplomacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a founding member of the <b>Association of Southeast Asian Nations</b> (ASEAN) in 1967, whose Kuala Lumpur declaration of 1971 proposed the region as a Zone of Peace, Freedom and Neutrality, and it has been active in the Commonwealth, the Non-Aligned Movement and the Organisation of Islamic…</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…1988 uprising and the long detention of Aung San Suu Kyi, who received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1991, made Burma a focus of Western sanctions and human rights campaigns, while China, India and the <b>Association of Southeast Asian Nations</b> (ASEAN), which Myanmar joined in 1997, pursued engagement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A founding member of <b>ASEAN</b> in 1967, Singapore hosts the secretariats of APEC and the Pacific Economic Cooperation Council, belongs to the Commonwealth, the Non-Aligned Movement and the East Asia Summit and keeps diplomatic relations with more than 180 states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
